      "latency_ms": 4819.5,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 561,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on the surface of Earth. It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View (What it’s made of)\n* **Chemical Formula:** $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$\n* This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **The Bonds:** The atoms are held together by covalent bonds, but water molecules also form \"hydrogen bonds\" with each other. This creates a sort of molecular \"stickiness,\" which is why water beads up into drops and has high surface tension.\n\n### 2. The Physical States (How it looks and acts)\nWater is unique because, under normal Earth conditions, it is the only natural substance that exists abundantly in three distinct states of matter:\n* **Liquid:** The standard, flowing water we drink, swim in, and find in oceans and rivers.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water. Uniquely, solid water is *less* dense than liquid water, which is why ice floats.\n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water gas in the air (humidity) or steam rising from boiling water.\n\n### 3. Unique Properties\nWater has several remarkable scientific properties that make life possible:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is how our blood transports nutrients throughout our bodies.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat without drastically changing its own temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps human body temperatures stable.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water sticks to itself (cohesion—creating raindrops) and sticks to other things (adhesion—allowing water to travel up the roots of giant trees against gravity).\n\n### 4. Ecological and Biological Importance\n* **On Earth:** About 71% of the Earth's surface is covered by water, mostly in oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n* **In Living Things:** Your body is made of about 60% water. It acts as a shock absorber for the brain and spinal cord, regulates body temperature through sweat, and flushes out waste. Without water, biochemical reactions within cells cannot take place, and life ceases. \n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that acts as the lifeblood of our planet and all living organisms."
